= Belief revision =

== Overview ==
'''Belief revision''' is the formal study of how a body of beliefs should change when information is added, removed, or conflicts with existing commitments.

The field is central to formal epistemology and knowledge representation.

== Belief states ==
A common formal representation treats an agent's beliefs as sentences in a logical language.

The classic AGM framework represents a belief state as a logically closed belief set.

Other approaches use finite or non-closed belief bases.

== Expansion ==
'''Expansion''' adds a new belief without removing old beliefs.

If the new belief conflicts with the existing set, simple expansion can produce inconsistency.

== Contraction ==
'''Contraction''' removes commitment to a specified belief while changing the remaining state as little as the framework allows.

== Revision ==
'''Revision''' incorporates new information while restoring or preserving consistency when the input conflicts with existing beliefs.

A central AGM construction relates revision to contraction through the Levi identity.

== Minimal change ==
Belief-revision systems try to preserve as much prior information as possible while satisfying rationality constraints.

How minimal change should be defined is a major theoretical question.

== Beyond AGM ==
Contemporary belief-change research includes:

* belief bases;
* iterated revision;
* non-prioritized revision;
* merging multiple sources;
* dynamic epistemic approaches;
* connections to probabilities.

AGM is foundational but is not the whole field.

== Belief revision and Bayesian inference ==
AGM-style revision primarily models full beliefs.

[[Bayesian inference]] models graded probabilities or credences.

The frameworks can be connected, but:

<pre>
belief revision ≠ Bayesian conditionalization
</pre>

== Related concepts ==
[[Belief]], [[Knowledge]], [[Inference]], and [[Consistency (logic)]] provide the epistemic and logical background for formal belief change.

